.TH READ 5
.SH NAME
read, write, readdir \- transfer data or entries
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B size[4] Tread tag[2] fid[4] offset[8] count[4]
.B size[4] Rread tag[2] count[4] data[count]
.fi
.PP
.nf
.B size[4] Twrite tag[2] fid[4] offset[8] count[4] data[count]
.B size[4] Rwrite tag[2] count[4]
.fi
.PP
.nf
.B size[4] Treaddir tag[2] fid[4] offset[8] count[4]
.B size[4] Rreaddir tag[2] count[4] data[count]
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Tread
asks for
.I count
bytes from the file
.I fid
names, starting
.I offset
bytes into it.
.I Fid
must already be open; a read of a fid before
.B Tlopen
is refused with
.BR EINVAL .
.I Count
in the reply may be less than what was asked for, and zero bytes
with no error is the end of the file.
.PP
.B Twrite
asks that
.I count
bytes at
.I data
be written at
.I offset
in the file
.IR fid
names, and the reply's
.I count
is how many actually were.
A caller that sends fewer than it meant to has a short write to
account for; it is not itself an error.
.PP
Neither message may serialise past the session's
.IR msize .
A bulk caller chunks by
.IR iounit ,
described in
.IR open (5),
which is never larger than
.I msize
less
.BR IOHDRSZ .
.PP
.B Treaddir
reads directory entries rather than raw bytes, and
.I fid
must be open on a directory.
.I Offset
is an opaque cookie: it must be zero, or a value an earlier
.B Rreaddir
entry returned, never an arbitrary byte position.
.I Data
holds as many whole entries as fit in
.IR count ,
each shaped
.BR qid[13] ,
.BR offset[8] ,
.BR type[1] ,
.BR name[s] .
.I Type
is a Linux dirent type: 4 for a directory, 8 for a plain file.
Zero entries with no error is the end of the directory.
.PP
A directory presented as a union of several trees, bound together
under one name, concatenates its members' entries in mount order and
does not filter duplicates: a name two members share is listed
twice, though opening it by name reaches only the first.
Such a union, and
.BR /srv ,
hide which member an entry belongs to in the high bits of its
.I offset
rather than in a byte position, so a listing resumes at the right
member even after one earlier in the order was added or removed.
See
.IR vfs (9).
.SH SOURCE
.B sys/vectra9/proto.odin
.br
.B sys/vectra9/codec.odin
.br
.B kernel/vfs/chan.odin
.br
.B kernel/vfs/readdir.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR intro (5),
.IR open (5),
.IR flush (5),
.IR getattr (5),
.IR vectra9 (2),
.IR vfs (9)
